<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">

  <Package Name="Keysharp"
           Manufacturer="Keysharp"
           Version="$(KeysharpMsiVersion)"
           UpgradeCode="E99D4BE3-7101-4C11-8719-A75ED137BD66"
           Language="1033"
           Codepage="1252"
           InstallerVersion="500"
           Compressed="yes"
           Scope="perMachine">

    <!-- The UpgradeCode is carried over verbatim from the Visual Studio setup project. It is the only
         identifier that ties this package to every previously shipped Keysharp MSI; changing it would make
         a new release install alongside an old one instead of upgrading it. -->

    <!-- Replaces the two hand-rolled Upgrade rows, the ERRCA_CANCELNEWERVERSION action, and the post-build
         re-sequencing of RemoveExistingProducts that package-windows.ps1 used to inject.
         AllowSameVersionUpgrades: the build stamps a fresh ProductCode every time, so without it two builds
         of the same version install side by side and both appear in Programs and Features.
         afterInstallInitialize: the old product is fully removed before InstallFiles runs, so InstallFiles
         cannot skip a same-version file that the subsequent removal then deletes. -->
    <MajorUpgrade AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
                  Schedule="afterInstallInitialize"
                  DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed. Setup will now exit." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" CompressionLevel="high" />

    <!-- ==================== Closing running Keysharp processes ====================
         Windows refuses to overwrite a running image or a loaded DLL, so an upgrade or uninstall performed
         while Keysharp is running fails to replace or delete Keysharp.exe / Keysharp.Core.dll.

         The vdproj could not sequence a custom action before InstallValidate, so package-windows.ps1 built
         the MSI and then injected a VBScript action into its tables with the WindowsInstaller COM API.
         Two mechanisms replace it here.

         The Restart Manager, enabled for free by InstallerVersion 500, handles the graceful path. It scopes
         itself perfectly - it looks only at processes holding the files THIS package installs, so a second
         Keysharp installation elsewhere is untouched - and it drives the standard MsiRMFilesInUse dialog.

         The Restart Manager alone is NOT enough, which a real uninstall proved: it correctly detected the
         compile daemon and reported it, then could not close it, and the uninstall failed until the daemon
         was killed by hand. The reason is structural - the Restart Manager shuts an application down by
         *asking* it to exit (WM_QUERYENDSESSION / WM_ENDSESSION to top-level windows, or a service stop).
         The compile daemon is a background process with no window and no message loop, so there is nothing
         to ask, and Windows Installer does not pass RmForceShutdown. Detected-but-unclosable is precisely
         the case the Restart Manager reports and then leaves alone.

         So util:CloseApplication supplies the force-kill. WixCloseApplications is a native custom action
         from WixToolset.Util, a supported component - not the deprecated VBScript host that WIX1163 warns
         about, which is why the old script was not ported verbatim.

         Accepted limitation: util:CloseApplication matches on image name only, so unlike the old script it
         cannot restrict itself to processes under this install directory, and a second Keysharp
         installation would also be closed. That is why it was rejected on the first pass - but an
         uninstall that cannot complete is a worse defect than an over-broad close during an uninstall, and
         the directory scoping was defensive polish rather than a reported scenario.

         These live under Package, not in a Fragment: an unreferenced Fragment is silently discarded, which
         produced a perfectly clean build whose MSI contained no close logic at all. -->
    <util:CloseApplication Id="CloseKeysharp"
                           Target="Keysharp.exe"
                           Description="Keysharp is running and must be closed before Setup can continue."
                           CloseMessage="yes"
                           TerminateProcess="0"
                           RebootPrompt="no"
                           Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot; OR WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED" />
    <util:CloseApplication Id="CloseKeyview"
                           Target="Keyview.exe"
                           Description="The Keyview editor is running and must be closed before Setup can continue."
                           CloseMessage="yes"
                           TerminateProcess="0"
                           RebootPrompt="no"
                           Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot; OR WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED" />

    <!-- WixToolset.Util schedules WixCloseApplications at 3999, just before InstallFiles. That suits an
         install, but on an UNINSTALL it lands after RemoveFiles (3500), so the files are still locked when
         Windows Installer tries to delete them.

         It must therefore move earlier - but NOT before InstallInitialize. WixCloseApplications is an
         immediate action whose real work is done by a companion DEFERRED action it schedules through
         MsiDoAction, and a deferred action can only be written into the execution script once
         InstallInitialize has opened the transaction. Scheduling it before InstallValidate, where the old
         injected VBScript ran, aborts every uninstall with error 2762, "cannot write script record,
         transaction not started". (The old script could sit there because it was immediate and did the
         killing itself; this one cannot.) A fresh install was unaffected only because the condition below
         is false unless something is being removed.

         So it goes in the earliest legal slot: just after InstallInitialize opens the transaction and just
         before RemoveExistingProducts, giving 1500 / 1501 / 1502. Ordering ahead of
         RemoveExistingProducts matters on an upgrade - that action takes the older build apart, and its
         files are still locked if the old daemon has not been closed yet. Actions between
         InstallInitialize and InstallFinalize are written to the script in sequence order and executed in
         that order, so this also lands before RemoveFiles (3500).

         InstallValidate (1400) still runs first and may raise the in-use dialog. For a daemon that answers
         shutdown requests (see StartShutdownListener in CompileServer.cs) the Restart Manager resolves it
         there and this action finds nothing left to do; for an older daemon the dialog appears and this
         action is what actually clears the lock. -->
    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <Custom Action="override Wix4CloseApplications_$(UtilCaSuffix)" Before="RemoveExistingProducts" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>


    <SummaryInformation Description="Keysharp - a cross-platform C# port of AutoHotkey v2"
                        Manufacturer="Keysharp" />

    <!-- Add/Remove Programs metadata (the vdproj's ARP* properties). -->
    <Icon Id="Keysharp.ico" SourceFile="$(PayloadDir)\Keysharp.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="Keysharp.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPCONTACT" Value="Matt Feemster, Descolada et al" />
    <Property Id="ARPCOMMENTS" Value="A cross platform C# port of AutoHotkey V2" />
    <Property Id="ARPURLINFOABOUT" Value="https://github.com/keysharp-org/keysharp" />
    <!-- No ARPNOREPAIR here. Windows Installer keys the ARP "Repair" button off that property's PRESENCE,
         not its value, so setting it to 0 does not "enable repair" - it risks disabling it. The vdproj
         package never set it either. -->
    <SetProperty Id="ARPINSTALLLOCATION" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]" After="CostFinalize" Sequence="both" />

    <!-- ============================ .NET prerequisite ============================
         The publish is framework-dependent. Without the runtime the apphost shows Microsoft's own
         "install the .NET Desktop Runtime" dialog on first launch, so this check is not the difference
         between working and silently failing - it moves the diagnosis to install time.

         That framing matters, because the error asymmetry here is brutal: failing to detect a present
         runtime HARD-BLOCKS a completely valid install, while failing to notice an absent one merely
         defers to Microsoft's dialog, which offers a download link. So this deliberately detects only
         "no Windows Desktop runtime is installed at all" and does NOT try to assert a minimum version.

         An earlier attempt read a Version value from
         ...\InstalledVersions\<arch>\sharedfx\Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App and required it to contain
         "10.". That key does not exist on machines whose .NET arrived with the SDK or Visual Studio - it
         was absent on a machine running Desktop Runtime 10.0.9 - so it blocked a perfectly good install.
         Per-runtime versions are not reliably discoverable from the registry; do not reintroduce a
         version assertion here. Gating on a specific version is what a Burn bundle is for, since a bundle
         can install the runtime rather than merely refusing.

         sharedhost is written by the .NET host itself on every install path, and its Path value points at
         the real dotnet root - so anchoring the directory search there also handles a non-default install
         location, which a hard-coded [ProgramFiles64Folder] would not.

         The registry search alone is still not quite enough: dotnet-install.ps1 and DOTNET_ROOT layouts
         write no InstalledVersions key at all, and would be hard-blocked despite being perfectly able to
         run Keysharp. The second search covers that by looking in the default location directly. A machine
         with no .NET whatsoever satisfies neither and is correctly blocked; anything that satisfies either
         is let through, which is the right way round given the asymmetry above. -->
    <Property Id="NETDESKTOPRUNTIME">
      <RegistrySearch Id="DotnetSharedHostPath"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="SOFTWARE\dotnet\Setup\InstalledVersions\$(NetRegArch)\sharedhost"
                      Name="Path"
                      Type="directory"
                      Bitness="always64">
        <DirectorySearch Id="WindowsDesktopAppDir" Path="shared\Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App" />
      </RegistrySearch>
    </Property>
    <Property Id="NETDESKTOPRUNTIMEDIR">
      <DirectorySearch Id="DotnetDefaultRoot" Path="[ProgramFiles64Folder]dotnet">
        <DirectorySearch Id="WindowsDesktopAppDefaultDir" Path="shared\Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App" />
      </DirectorySearch>
    </Property>
    <Launch Condition="Installed OR NETDESKTOPRUNTIME OR NETDESKTOPRUNTIMEDIR"
            Message="[ProductName] needs the .NET Desktop Runtime ($(NetRegArch)), and no version of it was found on this machine. Install .NET Desktop Runtime 10 from https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download/dotnet/10.0 and run Setup again." />

    <!-- Remember where this was installed, so an upgrade stays put. Without it, a user who installed to a
         non-default folder gets silently relocated back to Program Files by the next version -
         RemoveExistingProducts then dismantles the old location - and a /qn upgrade gives no chance to
         notice. The vdproj had the same gap, but this package offers a folder browser, so honouring the
         choice matters more. The search only fires when the value exists, so a fresh install still takes
         the Directory default below. -->
    <Property Id="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <RegistrySearch Id="PreviousInstallFolder"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\Keysharp\Keysharp"
                      Name="InstallFolder"
                      Type="raw"
                      Bitness="always64" />
    </Property>

    <!-- ============================ Install scope ============================
         Per-machine, and ONLY per-machine.

         Q1 asked for a per-machine default plus an optional per-user install needing no administrator
         rights. WiX v5 cannot express that, and the attempt shipped a broken installer, so it is dropped:

           * Package/@Scope accepts only perMachine and perUser; perUserOrPerMachine arrived in v6.
           * Declaring perUser to dodge the forced ALLUSERS=1 sets bit 3 of the summary WordCount,
             "elevated privileges are not required". Windows Installer takes that literally and never
             elevates - so a per-machine install writing to Program Files and HKLM fails outright, with no
             UAC prompt and no fallback to a per-user install. That is exactly what happened in testing.
           * Declaring perMachine and overriding ALLUSERS to 2 by hand does not compile: perMachine emits
             its own ALLUSERS symbol and WiX rejects the duplicate.

         So the classic dual-purpose ALLUSERS=2 model has no supported expression in WiX v5. A working
         per-machine installer beats a clever broken one, and the no-administrator case is already served
         by the portable ZIP. If a real per-user install is wanted later, WiX v6's perUserOrPerMachine is
         the clean route - not a hand-rolled ALLUSERS hack.

         perMachine also means the summary WordCount elevation bit is clear, so Setup prompts for UAC. -->

    <!-- ============================ Directories ============================
         Scripts\, Lib\ and runtimes\<rid>\native\ are created implicitly by the payload harvest. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Keysharp" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="ShortcutFolder" Name="Keysharp" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!-- ============================ Features ============================
         Splitting PATH and shell integration out of the always-installed core is what replaces the vdproj's
         single "Add to path" checkbox. Unlike that checkbox - which a silent install could not turn off,
         because its SetProperty action re-set CHECKBOXREG=1 in the execute sequence - these respond to
         ADDLOCAL/REMOVE, so `msiexec /qn ADDLOCAL=Core` really does install neither. -->
    <Feature Id="Core" Title="Keysharp" Description="Keysharp, the Keyview editor and the bundled scripts." Level="1" AllowAbsent="no" Display="expand" ConfigurableDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="PayloadComponents" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ShortcutComponents" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="DocumentationComponents" />

      <Feature Id="PathEntry" Title="Add to PATH" Description="Add the install folder to PATH, so scripts can be run as `keysharp` from a command line." Level="1">
        <ComponentGroupRef Id="PathComponents" />
      </Feature>

      <Feature Id="ShellIntegration" Title="Shell integration" Description="Associate .ks and .cks files with Keysharp, and add the Compile and Edit with Keyview context-menu commands." Level="1">
        <ComponentGroupRef Id="ShellIntegrationComponents" />
      </Feature>
    </Feature>

    <!-- ============================ User interface ============================
         Welcome -> Licence -> Customize -> Confirm -> Progress -> Finish.

         CustomizeDlg carries both the feature tree and a folder browser, which together restore the two
         things the vdproj's UI offered: an install-location choice and an opt-out for adding Keysharp to
         PATH. The browser is driven by Core/@ConfigurableDirectory above, not by WIXUI_INSTALLDIR, which
         belongs to WixUI_InstallDir and WixUI_Advanced and would be inert here.

         The other dialog sets were each tried and rejected: WixUI_Advanced only made sense while the
         package was dual-scope, for its per-user/per-machine chooser, and has no feature page at all;
         WixUI_InstallDir has the folder page but no feature tree; WixUI_Mondo has both, but fronts them
         with a Typical/Custom/Complete page whose Typical and Complete are indistinguishable here - every
         feature is Level=1, so both install everything. -->
    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_FeatureTree" />

    <!-- Offer to open the Dash from the last page, ticked by default. WixShellExec runs in the installer's
         UI process, which is the user's ordinary unelevated token - a deferred action would launch the
         launcher as SYSTEM. Return="ignore" because a failure to start it is not a failed install.
         The shared WixUI ExitDialog hides its checkbox unless the text property is set and this is a fresh
         install, so repair, modify and uninstall never offer it; a silent install has no ExitDialog at all. -->
    <Property Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOXTEXT" Value="Open Keysharp" />
    <Property Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX" Value="1" />
    <CustomAction Id="LaunchKeysharp" BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_$(UtilCaSuffix)" DllEntry="WixShellExec"
                  Impersonate="yes" Return="ignore" />
    <UI>
      <!-- The target is set from the button rather than as a <Property>, for two reasons: a Property value
           is authored, not formatted, so [INSTALLFOLDER] would be passed literally (WIX1077); and setting
           it here reads INSTALLFOLDER after the user has picked a folder in CustomizeDlg, which a
           SetProperty scheduled after CostFinalize would miss. -->
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Order="1"
               Property="WixShellExecTarget" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]Keysharp.exe"
               Condition="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX = 1 and NOT Installed" />
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Order="2" Event="DoAction" Value="LaunchKeysharp"
               Condition="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX = 1 and NOT Installed" />
    </UI>

    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="$(ProjectDir)assets\license.rtf" />
    <WixVariable Id="WixUIBannerBmp" Value="$(ProjectDir)assets\banner.bmp" />
    <WixVariable Id="WixUIDialogBmp" Value="$(ProjectDir)assets\dialog.bmp" />

  </Package>

  <Fragment>
    <!-- README.md is the one installed file that does not come from the staged publish tree, so it is
         authored explicitly rather than harvested. The vdproj also installed assets\InstallerBanner.bmp
         into the application folder; that is a Setup UI asset and is deliberately not shipped any more. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="DocumentationComponents" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component>
        <File Source="$(RepoRoot)README.md" Name="README.md" />
      </Component>

      <!-- Records the chosen location for the next version's INSTALLFOLDER search, so an upgrade installs
           where this one did rather than snapping back to Program Files. -->
      <Component Id="RecordInstallFolder">
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="Software\Keysharp\Keysharp" Name="InstallFolder" Type="string" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment>
    <!-- Non-advertised shortcuts, targeted by path rather than by file key, so the payload can stay a
         wholesale harvest with no hand-maintained file IDs. The vdproj's shortcuts were advertised, which
         made Windows run a repair check on every click; these behave like ordinary shortcuts.

         Exactly the two entries every other edition offers - the MSIX tiles, the Linux .desktop files,
         the two macOS .app bundles. Window Spy had a third shortcut here and nowhere else; it is reached
         from the Dash, like the demos and the documentation. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="ShortcutComponents" Directory="ShortcutFolder">
      <Component>
        <!-- Plain "Keysharp", the same entry every other edition offers (the MSIX tile, the Linux
             .desktop, Keysharp.app on macOS) - not a second "Dash" entry beside it. No arguments on
             purpose: started with no script, Keysharp falls back to the bundled Dash,
             Scripts\Dash.cks, produced by the PrecompileBundledScripts target. So the launcher needs
             no path baked into any package. -->
        <Shortcut Id="KeysharpShortcut"
                  Name="Keysharp"
                  Description="Keysharp: new script, run a script, tools and demos"
                  Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]Keysharp.exe"
                  WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                  Icon="Keysharp.ico" />
        <Shortcut Id="KeyviewShortcut"
                  Name="Keyview"
                  Description="The Keysharp script editor"
                  Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]Keyview.exe"
                  WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                  Icon="Keysharp.ico" />
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveShortcutFolder" On="uninstall" />
        <!-- Shortcut components must key off HKCU, not HKMU: the Start Menu folder is per-user data, and
             ICE57 rejects pairing that with a keypath that could resolve to HKLM. The vdproj's shortcut
             component did the same thing (its Registry rows were HKCU). -->
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU" Key="Software\Keysharp\Keysharp" Name="Shortcuts" Type="integer" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>

</Wix>
